      "keyInsight": "Ask ten expert drivers to write down the exact mathematical physics of steering around a slick curve. Most will stall out or hand you totally contradictory lists of abstract formulas. Yet every single one of them steers safely through heavy rain every single day. We assume communities run on thick legal rulebooks carved into stone tablets. In reality, shared traditions work through muscle memory and shared master models. You learn a craft by watching master craftspeople shape wood on a lathe, not by memorizing a legal charter. Shared examples guide action long before anybody manages to write down neat definitions. Shared practice unites the workshop far better than rigid words ever could.",
      "content": "Think about how you learned to ride a bicycle down your neighborhood street. Nobody handed you a three-ring binder covering gravitational torque, angular momentum, and balance equations. A family member held the back of the seat, ran alongside you, and let go. You wobbled, corrected your lean, fell into the grass, and quickly tried again. Within three afternoons, the feel of balance lived permanently in your legs and hands.\n\nProfessional fields guide their workers through the exact same kind of hands-on tradition. Long before a newcomer steps up to an advanced workbench, they spend years recreating famous, classic benchmarks. They run classic demonstrations, measure classic results, and calibrate their tools against beloved standard models. They absorb the unspoken rhythm of the field through concrete practice rather than abstract philosophy. They know what a solid, reliable piece of work looks like because they have built copies of it with their own hands.\n\nThis explains why sharp researchers can agree on every practical step while disagreeing on exact definitions. Two seasoned chefs might argue endlessly about what technically defines a soup versus a stew. Yet when dinner rush hits, both chop vegetables, season broth, and send out delicious meals without hesitation. Concrete models hold communities together when rigid rulebooks fail.",
